 # Hierarchical and Distributed Optimization
+## Overview
+Hierarchical and Distributed Optimization refers to strategies for scaling Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) training to large-scale scenes or correcting imperfect camera poses by decomposing the optimization problem [G]. It addresses the limitations of standard single-stage optimization, which fails with large initial pose errors [8223-cascaded-bundle-adjusting-neural-radiance-fields-cbarf.md] or suffers from memory bottlenecks and blending artifacts when scaling to arbitrarily large scenes [10947-nerf-xl-joint-training.md]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like BlockNeRF or MegaNeRF by either using a cascaded coarse-to-fine pose refinement strategy [8223-cascaded-bundle-adjusting-neural-radiance-fields-cbarf.md] or by enforcing 0% overlap with joint training of global embeddings to eliminate train-test discrepancies [10947-nerf-xl-joint-training.md].
+## Shared principles
+The family relies on the differentiability of the forward rendering operator and the propagation of gradients through the integration steps [G]. The core mechanism is the evaluation of the volume rendering equation and the computation of gradients with respect to scene parameters (density, geometry, transfer functions, camera poses) [G].
 
